WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.221
Step 3: axial coding into a failure taxonomy.

2
00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:07.427
When you have notes on enough failures, read them all and group similar ones.

3
00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:12.229
This is axial coding: finding the categories that connect your open codes.

4
00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:23.261
The output is a failure taxonomy, a short list of failure modes, each with a one-line definition precise enough that two people would usually agree on whether a trace shows it.

5
00:00:23.541 --> 00:00:30.709
The FAQ calls this grouping step the most important part, and the next step is to count failures in each category.

6
00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:35.021
A good taxonomy for one product usually has five to ten modes.

7
00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:40.891
Too few, and a category like “wrong answer” hides two different problems with two different fixes.

8
00:00:41.171 --> 00:00:43.475
Too many, and you’re back to individual notes.

9
00:00:43.755 --> 00:00:48.533
Write the definitions down, because they become the spec for the graders you’ll build next week.

10
00:00:48.813 --> 00:00:52.611
An LLM can help here, after you’ve done the first pass yourself.

11
00:00:52.891 --> 00:01:03.707
The FAQ suggests annotating at least 30 traces by hand before letting a model cluster notes or suggest matches, and then accepting or rejecting its suggestions.

12
00:01:03.987 --> 00:01:13.075
In the video they explain why the first pass should be a person: the model doesn’t know your product’s rules, and the act of reading is how you learn what failure means.

13
00:01:13.355 --> 00:01:22.507
Model choice matters little for this clustering step; any strong general model with enough context to hold your notes will do, and you are the check on its groupings.

14
00:01:22.787 --> 00:01:27.907
Keep going until new traces stop producing new codes or changing your definitions.

15
00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:41.712
The FAQ calls this theoretical saturation, and treats it as the signal to stop the discovery phase for this cycle (it suggests reviewing at least 100 traces and continuing past that while you’re still learning).
